<h1>Fetch</h1>
<hr align="left" width="98.974%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p><span lang="EN-GB">This command is accessible from</span> <i style="font-style:normal;"><span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Edit &gt; Fetch</b></span> </i><span lang="EN-GB">or by pressing</span> <span class="Shortcut" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Ctrl + Shift + F</b></span><span lang="EN-GB">.</span></p>
<p class="Description"><span class="Command" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Decsription:</b></span> <span class="Command">The Fetch</span> <span lang="EN-GB">command restores a previous state of data in the scene. It works as "multiple steps" Undo command, but performed at once. The command is grayed when there is no saved file state. You cannot Fetch File operations and operations in Inspection mode.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>How the command works</b></span><span lang="EN-GB">: When a data is saved using the Hold command, its state can be retrieved at any time the user wants, regardless of the meanwhile performed operations. You can save, respectively retrieve, one data state at a time. If you need to keep your data before each processing step, then better enable the Undo option.</span></p>
<p class="Use"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Use</b></span>: This command is very useful to reject multiple operations at once.</p>
